WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.540
RL: We know by now that glycolysis takes place in the cytoplasm,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.060
and leads to the formation of two molecules of pyruvate

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.080
per molecule of glucose.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:20.610
These pyruvate molecules are converted into acetyl coenzyme A, also known

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:25.740
as acetyl-CoA, and this happens inside the mitochondrial matrix.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.870
Therefore, acetyl-CoA is the key metabolic intermediate

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:33.670
that links glycolysis and the citric acid cycle.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.580
For clarity, we will not show the coenzyme portion of acetyl-CoA,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:44.090
and instead are simply representing it as the two-carbon molecule acetate.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:49.020
So how does the pyruvate get into the mitochondrial matrix?

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.430
This depends on a carrier protein that transports

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.020
pyruvate into the mitochondrial matrix, where there it

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.980
is oxidized to form acetyl-CoA by the enzyme pyruvate dehydrogenase.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.430
In this reaction, a molecule of carbon dioxide is generated as pyruvate is

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:13.280
oxidized to acetyl-CoA, and a molecule of NAD+ is reduced to NADH.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.190
This last reaction is not shown for clarity.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:21.070
The citric acid cycle consists of the following eight steps,

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:24.270
and also harvests chemical energy, producing

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:27.810
a total of three NADH molecules.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.430
In the first step of the citric acid cycle,

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.850
the enzyme citrate synthase combines the two-carbon acetyl group

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:40.020
from acetyl-CoA with the four-carbon molecule oxaloacetate.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.620
This generates the six-carbon molecule citrate.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.360
In the second step, citrate is isomerized into isocitrate

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.020
by aconitase.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:55.400
In the third step, isocitrate is oxidized by isocitrate dehydrogenase,

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.660
generating the five-carbon molecule alpha-ketoglutarate.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.220
In the process, one carbon dioxide molecule is released,

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:07.850
and one NAD+ molecule is reduced to NADH.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.669
In the fourth step, alpha-ketoglutarate is

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:16.500
decarboxylated and oxidized to produce another molecule of carbon dioxide

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.560
by alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:25.550
The resulting molecule is combined with coenzyme A, forming succinyl-CoA.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:32.120
In addition, another molecule of NAD+ is reduced to form NADH.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.700
Note that, in this diagram, we are showing

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.460
the product of this reaction as succinate,

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.690
and are omitting the coenzyme A for clarity.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:45.800
In the fifth step, succinyl coenzyme A is converted into succinate

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:51.750
by succinyl-CoA synthetase, releasing coenzyme A. This reaction also

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:57.150
produces one molecule of GTP, another high-energy molecule, which is

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.930
omitted from this diagram for clarity.

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:07.100
In the sixth step, succinate is oxidized to fumarate by succinate dehydrogenase.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.290
Unlike all the other enzymes in the citric acid cycle,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:14.990
succinate dehydrogenase is embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane,

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.460
and is also a component of the electron transport chain,

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.560
in which it is also called Complex II.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.870
As Complex II oxidizes succinate, it receives two electrons

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.690
that it will ultimately pass on to coenzyme Q, which in turn will

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:35.750
deliver its electrons to Complex III.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:42.760
Another interesting aspect of Complex II is that it uses FAD rather than NAD+

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:44.980
to accept electrons.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:47.770
In the seventh step, fumarate is converted

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.810
to malate by the enzyme fumarase.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:56.430
And finally, in the eighth step, the enzyme malate dehydrogenase oxidizes

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:03.750
malate to re-form oxaloacetate, and reduces a molecule of NAD+ to NADH

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.250
in the process.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:11.180
This regenerated oxaloacetate can then combine with another acetyl-CoA

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.330
in the next cycle.

